/*===============================================================================
📘 SYSTEMVERILOG ASSERTIONS — $rose() and $fell()
===============================================================================

🔹 1. WHY WE USE $rose() AND $fell()
When a signal (like `req`) stays high for many clock cycles, a normal assertion like:
req |-> ##[1:3] ack;
starts multiple threads every cycle while req = 1.
This can create false failures even if the design is correct.

✅ To fix this, we use event-based triggers:
$rose(req) → checks when req rises (0 → 1)
$fell(req) → checks when req falls (1 → 0)
These start only once per event, not every clock cycle.


🔹 2. SIMPLE EXAMPLE
property p_req_ack;
  @(posedge clk) disable iff (!reset_n)
  $rose(req) |-> ##[1:3] ack;
endproperty

assert property (p_req_ack)
  else $error("ACK not received within 3 cycles after REQ");

✅ Meaning:
When req rises, ack must go high within 1 to 3 clock cycles.
Assertion ignores reset using disable iff (!reset_n).


🔹 3. QUICK NOTES
Function	                       Meaning
$rose(signal)	          Triggers once when signal goes 0→1
$fell(signal)	          Triggers once when signal goes 1→0
`	->`
##[m:n]	Delay of m to n cycles
disable iff(cond)	Turns off assertion when condition is true


🔹 4. EXAMPLE TIMING
clk: ↑   ↑   ↑   ↑   ↑
req: 0   1   1   1   0
ack: 0   0   0   1   0

Without $rose(req) → 3 checks start → false fails ❌
With $rose(req) → Only one check starts → correct ✅


🔹 5. REAL USE
Used by Design Verification Engineers to:
Verify handshake signals (req–ack, valid–ready)
Ensure correct signal timing across cycles
Avoid false assertion failures

===============================================================================
